"marketSentiment.descriptions.composite.shortDescription" = "The Sentiment Inside Out Fear & Greed Indicator (SIO Fear & Greed Indicator) quantifies market sentiment into a score from 0 to 100. Its purpose is to gauge whether current market participants' psychology leans towards fear or greed, similar to the CNN Fear & Greed Index and the BofA Bull & Bear Indicator. It helps investors remain calm during irrational stock market volatility and avoid emotionally driven decisions like chasing highs and selling lows.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.composite.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Composite Index Explanation\", \"content\": \"The SIO Fear & Greed Indicator is a sentiment indicator calculated from a synthesis of multiple U.S. stock market data points, represented by a score from 0 to 100. A score closer to 0 signifies extreme fear in the market, potentially indicating a higher probability of success for buying at this time. Conversely, a score closer to 100 suggests extreme greed in the market, advising against chasing further highs. Compared to the CNN Fear & Greed Index, it incorporates a more comprehensive set of indicators, including the AAII Investor Sentiment Survey results—often considered a proxy for U.S. retail investor sentiment—to more accurately reflect the long-term relationship between market sentiment and prices.\"}, {\"title\": \"Historical Data Review\", \"content\": \"Historically, whenever the Sentiment Inside Out Fear & Greed Index (SIO Fear & Greed Index) reached around 10 points, it marked a bear market bottom (including the 2009 financial crisis, the 2020 COVID-19 pandemic, and the 2022 Russo-Ukrainian War). Seizing these moments presented excellent buying opportunities.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.aaiiSpread.shortDescription" = "The AAII Investor Sentiment Survey weekly asks members their outlook for the stock market over the next six months (bullish, bearish, neutral). The Bull-Bear Spread (bullish percentage minus bearish percentage) is a common measure of retail investor sentiment.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.aaiiSpread.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"An extremely high Bull-Bear Spread (well above the historical average) usually indicates excessive market optimism and could be a potential sell signal. Conversely, an extremely low Spread (well below the historical average, even negative) indicates excessive pessimism and could be a potential buying opportunity.\"}, {\"title\": \"Note\", \"content\": \"This indicator reflects retail sentiment, which is often considered a contrarian indicator. Professional investors might use extreme retail sentiment for counter-trading.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.cboeRatio.shortDescription" = "The CBOE Put/Call Ratio is an important indicator of market speculation sentiment. It calculates the ratio of traded put option contracts to call option contracts.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.cboeRatio.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"A high Put/Call Ratio (typically above 1) indicates investors are buying more puts for hedging or bearish bets, suggesting market fear. A low Put/Call Ratio (typically below 0.7) indicates investors are buying more calls for speculation or bullish bets, suggesting market greed. Using a 5-day average smooths the data for better trend observation.\"}, {\"title\": \"Contrarian Indicator\", \"content\": \"Similar to the AAII survey, extremely high Put/Call Ratios often occur at market bottoms, while extremely low ratios often occur at market tops, exhibiting contrarian characteristics.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.marketMomentum.shortDescription" = "The Market Momentum indicator measures the S&P 500 index's position relative to its 125-day moving average (approximately the half-year line). It reflects the strength of the market's medium-term trend.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.marketMomentum.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"When the S&P 500 is significantly above its 125-day MA, it indicates a strong uptrend, strong momentum, and market greed. When the index is significantly below the MA, it indicates a downtrend, weak momentum, and market fear.\"}, {\"title\": \"Combine with Other Indicators\", \"content\": \"Market momentum is a trend-following indicator and can be used in conjunction with other contrarian indicators (like the Put/Call Ratio) for a more comprehensive market assessment.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.vixMA50.shortDescription" = "The VIX Index (Volatility Index), often referred to as the 'Fear Gauge,' measures the market's expectation of 30-day forward-looking volatility of the S&P 500 Index. Here, the 50-day moving average of the VIX is calculated to observe long-term trends in market sentiment.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.vixMA50.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"An ascending VIX 50-day moving average indicates rising market fear and increased demand for hedging. Conversely, a descending VIX 50-day moving average suggests a relatively calm market, higher investor risk appetite, and a shift in market sentiment towards greed.\"}, {\"title\": \"Extreme Value Significance\", \"content\": \"Extreme spikes in the VIX often accompany significant market downturns and can be considered a contrarian buy signal. Persistently low VIX levels, on the other hand, may indicate excessive greed in the market, warranting caution\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.safeHaven.shortDescription" = "The Safe Haven Demand indicator compares the difference between the total return of stocks (risk asset) and US Treasury bonds (safe-haven asset)over the past 20 trading days. It reflects the extent of fund flows into safe-haven assets.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.safeHaven.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"When bond returns significantly outperform stock returns (low indicator value), it indicates investors are flocking to safe-haven assets, suggesting market fear. When stock returns significantly outperform bond returns (high indicator value), it indicates higher risk appetite and fund flows into risk assets, suggesting market greed.\"}, {\"title\": \"Observing Fund Flows\", \"content\": \"This indicator provides a window into changes in overall market risk appetite and is an important reference for judging market sentiment.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.junkBond.shortDescription" = "The yield spread between junk bonds (high-yield corporate bonds) and investment-grade corporate bonds reflects the market's attitude towards credit risk. Junk bonds are riskier and typically have higher yields.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.junkBond.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"When the spread widens (high indicator value), it means investors demand higher risk compensation to hold junk bonds. This usually happens when the market worries about economic prospects or tightening liquidity, indicating market fear. When the spread narrows (low indicator value), it means investors' risk tolerance increases, indicating market greed.\"}, {\"title\": \"Credit Market Signal\", \"content\": \"The junk bond spread is an important leading indicator for observing the health of the credit market and overall market risk appetite.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.cotIndex.shortDescription" = "The COT Index (Commitments of Traders Index), based on the weekly report from the CFTC, measures the net position (longs minus shorts) of large speculators (often considered more informed traders) in the S&P 500 futures market relative to historical levels.";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.cotIndex.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"A COT Index near 100% indicates large speculators' net long positions are at historical highs, suggesting the market might be overly bullish and leaning towards extreme greed. An index near 0% indicates net short positions are at historical highs (or net longs are extremely low), suggesting the market might be overly bearish and leaning towards extreme fear.\"}, {\"title\": \"Smart Money Movement\", \"content\": \"Tracking the position changes of large speculators helps understand the outlook of the so-called 'smart money' on future market direction.\"}]";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.naaimIndex.shortDescription" = "The NAAIM Exposure Index (National Association of Active Investment Managers Exposure Index) surveys its members (active investment managers) weekly on their current equity market exposure level (0% = no stocks, 100% = fully invested, >100% = leveraged).";
"marketSentiment.descriptions.naaimIndex.sections" = "[{\"title\": \"Indicator Interpretation\", \"content\": \"An extremely high exposure index (e.g., near or above 100%) indicates fund managers are generally bullish and actively holding stocks, suggesting market greed. An extremely low exposure index (e.g., near 0% or negative, indicating short positions) indicates managers are generally bearish and reducing stock holdings, suggesting market fear.\"}, {\"title\": \"Institutional Sentiment\", \"content\": \"This indicator reflects the sentiment and positioning adjustments of professional investment institutions, serving as an important reference for observing the mainstream market view.\"}]";
